Find the smallest positive interger number  $n$  such that there exists  $n$  real numbers  $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_n$  satisfied three conditions as follow:
a.  $a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n>0$ ;
b.  $a_1^3+a_2^3+\cdots+a_n^3<0$ ;
c.  $a_1^5+a_2^5+\cdots+a_n^5>0$ .

We consider the two sequences $\left(a_{n}\right)_{n \geq 0}$ and $\left(b_{n}\right)_{n \geq 0}$ of integers, which are defined by $a_{0}=b_{0}=2$ and $a_{1}=b_{1}=14$, and by

$$
\begin{aligned}
a_{n} & = 14 a_{n-1} + a_{n-2} \\
b_{n} & = 6 b_{n-1} - b_{n-2}
\end{aligned}
$$

for $n \geq 2$.

Determine whether there are infinitely many numbers that appear in both sequences.

(Gerhard Woeginger)

