Find the triplets $(p, q, n)$, where $p$ and $q$ are odd prime numbers, and $n$ is an integer greater than 1, such that:

$$
\begin{aligned}
q^{n+2} & \equiv 3^{n+2} \pmod{p^n}, \\
p^{n+2} & \equiv 3^{n+2} \pmod{q_n}
\end{aligned}
$$

Analyze the following code snippet and determine the final value of `k` in terms of `m` and `n`, where `x = 2^m` and `y = 2^n`:

```
i = 1;
j = y;
k = 0;
while (i < x) {
    i = i * 2;
    k++;
}
while (j > 1) {
    j = j / 2;
    k = k + j;
}
```

Using each of the following 4 numbers exactly once, arranged in any order, and using the operations "+", "-", "*", "/", and parentheses, make the number 24:
(1) 7, 12, 9, 12;
(2) 3, 9, 5, 9.

There is a bottle with 1001 blue balls, 1000 red balls, and 1000 green balls, and Xiao Ming has additional balls of these three colors. Then, Xiao Ming will take out two balls from the bottle each time and put one or two balls back into the bottle according to the following rules:
(1) If a blue ball and a green ball are taken out, put back one red ball;
(2) If a red ball and a green ball are taken out, put back one red ball;
(3) If two red balls are taken out, put back two blue balls;
(4) If the two balls taken out do not match any of the above three cases, put back one green ball.

The process continues until there is only one ball left in the bottle. What is the color of the remaining ball? (Fill in “red,” “blue,” “green,” or “uncertain”)

Let $\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of all polynomials in three variables $x, y, z$ with integer coefficients. Let $\mathcal{B}$ denote the subset of $\mathcal{A}$ formed by all polynomials which can be expressed as
\begin{align*}
(x + y + z)P(x, y, z) + (xy + yz + zx)Q(x, y, z) + xyzR(x, y, z)
\end{align*}
with $P, Q, R \in \mathcal{A}$.  Find the smallest non-negative integer $n$ such that $x^i y^j z^k \in \mathcal{B}$ for all non-negative integers $i, j, k$ satisfying $i + j + k \geq n$.

A semi-circle of radius 8 cm, rocks back and forth along a line.  The distance between the line on which the semi-circle sits and the line above is 12 cm.  As it rocks without slipping, the semi-circle touches the line above at two points.  (When the semi-circle hits the line above, it immediately rocks back in the other direction.)  What is the distance between these two points, in millimetres, rounded off to the nearest whole number? [asy]

draw((-15, -8)--(15, -8));draw((-15, 4)--(15, 4));draw((-8, 0)--(8, 0){down}..{up}(-8, 0));

[/asy] (Note: After finding the exact value of the desired distance, you may find a calculator useful to round this value off to the nearest whole number.)

